%S A401838 10,17,26,36,37,45,50,61,65,67,78,82,91,94,101,102,110,122,136,138,
%T A401838 145,149,153,155,165,170,173,181,183,188,197,210,213,220,226,231,232,
%U A401838 249,250,257,262,263,266,282,290,297,300,304,311,312,314,325,341,346,358
%N A401838 Numbers k such that two distinct integers can be removed from 1 through k so that their product equals the sum of the remaining integers.
%C A401838 Also, numbers k for which there exist distinct positive integers 1 <= c < d <= k satisfying c*d = T(k) - c - d or (c+1)*(d+1) = T(k)+1, where T(k) = k*(k+1)/2.
%C A401838 The latter provides a direct method for finding all solutions by examining factor pairs of T(k)+1. The first term with two distinct solutions is k = 325, with (c, d) = (171, 307) and (175, 300).
%H A401838 Michael S. Branicky, <a href="/A401838/b401838.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a> (first 200 terms from Pieter Post)
%e A401838 10 is a term since T(10) = 55, 55 - 6 - 7 = 42 and 42 = 6*7.
%e A401838 17 is a term since T(17) = 153, 153 - 10 - 13 = 130 = 10*13.
%o A401838 (Python)
%o A401838 from sympy import divisors
%o A401838 def ok(n):
%o A401838     t = n*(n+1)//2 + 1
%o A401838     return any(1 for d in divisors(t, generator=True) if (c:=t//d)!=d and max(c, d)<n)
%o A401838 print([k for k in range(360) if ok(k)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Nov 07 2026
%Y A401838 Cf. A000217, A401836, A401837.
%K A401838 nonn,new
%O A401838 1,1
%A A401838 _Pieter Post_, Nov 07 2026
